## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## Reckons.AI Docs — USER PATHS
## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## Matt, 2026-09-04: "We need to better explain the many different user paths, to
## utilize Reckons.AI — Building a graph for research, adding new content to an
## existing graph, comparing your graph to another's, viewing a story built on a
## graph, using reckoning, etc."
##
## WHY THIS IS A SEPARATE GRAPH FROM docs-features.ttl. That file lists 41
## features and answers "what can this do?" — organised by MECHANISM: Ingest,
## Review System, Compare, Reckoning, Shelly. A person arriving does not have a
## mechanism, they have an INTENTION: "I am researching something and want to
## keep track of what I learn." A feature list cannot answer that, because the
## answer is a sequence that crosses five features and skips thirty-six.
##
## So paths REFERENCE features rather than restating them (path:uses ->
## feat:Something). Editing a feature's description in one place stays correct in
## both, and a path that names no feature is a path with nothing behind it.
##
## EVERY PATH CARRIES ITS OWN HONEST STATUS (kb:honest-status). Some of these are
## smooth today; some have a real gap in the middle, and the gap is stated ON THE
## STEP where a person would hit it, not in a footnote at the bottom. A journey
## document that describes a frictionless flow which does not exist is the most
## expensive kind of lie this project can tell, because someone will follow it.
## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

@prefix rdf:    <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#> .
@prefix rdfs:   <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix xsd:    <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .
@prefix skos:   <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .
@prefix feat:   <urn:reckons:feature/> .
@prefix guide:  <urn:reckons:guide/> .
@prefix path:   <urn:reckons:path/> .
@prefix ktype:  <urn:kbase:type/> .
@prefix kpred:  <urn:kbase:predicate/> .
@prefix hnav:   <urn:reckons:nav/> .

<urn:reckons:kb> <urn:reckons:meta/kbStableId> "a1b2c3d4-e5f6-4a08-b008-000000000008" .

path:UserPaths
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    rdfs:label          "User paths" ;
    skos:definition     "The routes people actually take through Reckons.AI, each written as an intention rather than a feature. Start with the core loop below \u2014 every other path is that loop with one stage emphasised. They overlap deliberately, because the same person moves between them as their work changes." ;
    kpred:read-first    "IF YOU READ ONE THING, READ THE CORE LOOP. Everything here is the same four stages: something comes IN, the app PROPOSES what it thinks that means, you SETTLE what is true, and the settled graph is then USED \u2014 to decide, to compare, or to teach. A path is just that loop with the weight moved to a different stage." ;
    kpred:diagram       """flowchart TB
  subgraph s1["1 &middot; Capture"]
    N["A note, page, PDF,<br/>or something you said"]
  end
  subgraph s2["2 &middot; Propose"]
    E["Extraction"] --> P["Pending facts"]
  end
  subgraph s3["3 &middot; Settle"]
    R{"You review<br/>each claim"}
  end
  subgraph s4["4 &middot; Use"]
    G[("Your graph")]
    Q["Reckon<br/>decide something"]
    C["Compare<br/>against someone else"]
    S["Story<br/>teach someone"]
  end
  N --> E
  P --> R
  R -->|"confirm"| G
  R -->|"reject"| X["Dropped"]
  G --> Q
  G --> C
  G --> S""" ;
    kpred:diagram-caption "The four stages every user path passes through. Nothing reaches the graph without passing stage 3." ;
    kpred:principle     "A PATH IS NAMED BY WHAT THE PERSON WANTS, NOT BY WHAT THE APP PROVIDES. 'Building a graph for research' is a path; 'Ingest' is a feature. If a path's name could appear in the settings menu, it has been named from the wrong side." ;
    kpred:principle     "THE UNIT IS A TRIPLE, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE DIFFERENCE. A notes app stores what you wrote. This stores what you MEANT, broken into separate claims small enough to disagree with one at a time \u2014 subject, relation, object. That is why the graph can tell you two sources conflict, that a claim rests on nothing, or that a question was never answered. A paragraph cannot do any of those things, however well it is written." ;
    hnav:layer          "0"^^xsd:integer .

## ===========================================================================
## PATH 1 — Building a graph for research
## ===========================================================================

path:BuildForResearch
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    rdfs:label          "Building a graph for research" ;
    skos:definition     "You are investigating something over weeks — a technology choice, a literature area, a decision with real money behind it — and you want what you learn to accumulate instead of scattering across tabs and notes." ;
    kpred:audience      "Researcher, analyst, or anyone doing sustained investigation. The primary user this product is for." ;
    kpred:starts-with   "An empty graph and a question you cannot yet answer." ;
    kpred:ends-with     "A graph where the claims are separable, sourced, and disagreements are visible instead of averaged away." ;
    kpred:uses          feat:Ingest ;
    kpred:uses          feat:ReviewSystem ;
    kpred:uses          feat:TrustSystem ;
    kpred:uses          feat:Graph3D ;
    kpred:has-status    "functional" ;
    kpred:honest-note   "THE LONGEST PATH AND THE ONE WITH THE MOST FRICTION LEFT. Extraction and review both work; what does not yet work is the queue getting SHORTER as the graph grows. Measured 2026-09-04: a freshly extracted graph still presents as a flat list of ~32 claims with 0 grouped decisions, because typing proposes no types and hierarchy places no parents. You will get a good graph out of this path; you will spend longer in review than you should." ;
    kpred:diagram       """flowchart LR
  Q["A question you<br/>cannot answer yet"] --> N["New graph<br/>for that question"]
  N --> F["Feed it what<br/>you are reading"]
  F --> R{"Short review<br/>passes"}
  R --> G[("Claims, sourced<br/>and separable")]
  R --> M["Questions with<br/>no answer yet"]
  M -.->|"tells you where<br/>to look next"| F""" ;
    kpred:diagram-caption "Research is a loop, not a line. The unanswered questions feed back and direct the next source." ;
    skos:broader        path:UserPaths ;
    hnav:layer          "1"^^xsd:integer ;
    hnav:order          "3"^^xsd:integer .

path:Research-1
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    rdfs:label          "Start a graph for the question, not for everything" ;
    skos:definition     "Create a new graph per investigation rather than one graph for your whole life. Vocabulary repair and entity matching work by comparing new names against names the graph already holds, so a graph about one subject makes those matches meaningful and a graph about everything makes them noise." ;
    kpred:page          "/kb" ;
    kpred:part-of       path:BuildForResearch ;
    kpred:step-order    "1"^^xsd:integer .

path:Research-2
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    rdfs:label          "Feed it what you are already reading" ;
    skos:definition     "Paste a URL, drop a PDF, or dictate a note. Extraction turns prose into candidate triples. Nothing you add is trusted yet — every extracted fact arrives PENDING, which is the whole safety model: the app proposes, you settle." ;
    kpred:page          "/ingest" ;
    kpred:uses          feat:Ingest ;
    kpred:part-of       path:BuildForResearch ;
    kpred:step-order    "2"^^xsd:integer .

path:Research-3
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    rdfs:label          "Review in short passes, not one long one" ;
    skos:definition     "Settle facts while the source is still fresh in your mind. A reviewer who batches a week of ingests is judging claims they no longer remember reading, which is how a wrong fact gets confirmed." ;
    kpred:page          "/review" ;
    kpred:uses          feat:ReviewSystem ;
    kpred:part-of       path:BuildForResearch ;
    kpred:step-order    "3"^^xsd:integer ;
    kpred:honest-note   "This is where the friction is. See the path's honest-note: the queue is currently flat rather than grouped." .

path:Research-4
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    rdfs:label          "Let the shape of what is missing be a node" ;
    skos:definition     "When a source raises a question it does not answer, record the question. A partial fact — subject and predicate known, object open — is a first-class thing here, and it is what makes a graph tell you where to look next rather than only what you already found." ;
    kpred:uses          feat:ReviewSystem ;
    kpred:part-of       path:BuildForResearch ;
    kpred:step-order    "4"^^xsd:integer .

## ===========================================================================
## PATH 2 — Adding new content to an existing graph
## ===========================================================================

path:AddToExisting
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    rdfs:label          "Adding to a graph you already have" ;
    skos:definition     "The everyday path. The graph exists, you found something new, and the question is whether the new thing connects to what is already there or genuinely stands alone." ;
    kpred:audience      "Anyone returning to a graph they built before." ;
    kpred:starts-with   "An existing graph and one new source." ;
    kpred:ends-with     "New facts connected to the right existing entities — or standing as new ones, on purpose." ;
    kpred:uses          feat:Ingest ;
    kpred:uses          feat:Disambiguation ;
    kpred:uses          feat:ReviewSystem ;
    kpred:has-status    "functional" ;
    kpred:honest-note   "CONNECTION IS PARTLY AUTOMATIC AND PARTLY NOT YET SURFACED. Since 2026-09-04 extraction runs UNGROUNDED and reconciles afterwards: names that are the same word with different grammar (is-used-by / used-by) are connected automatically, and probable mis-hearings (lumen path / Lumenpath) are held back as proposals for you. The proposals are computed but have NO REVIEW SURFACE yet, so today you will meet both spellings as two separate rows and merge them by hand." ;
    kpred:diagram       """flowchart LR
  S["Something new"] --> D{"Does it belong to<br/>a graph you have?"}
  D -->|"yes"| E["Add there"]
  D -->|"no"| N["Its own graph"]
  E --> V["Read the diff,<br/>not the fact list"]
  V --> M{"Same thing,<br/>or genuinely new?"}""" ;
    kpred:diagram-caption "The everyday path. The real decision is not what to add but whether it is the same thing you already have." ;
    skos:broader        path:UserPaths ;
    hnav:layer          "1"^^xsd:integer ;
    hnav:order          "4"^^xsd:integer .

path:Add-1
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    rdfs:label          "Add into the graph the subject belongs to" ;
    skos:definition     "Switch to the right graph first. Ingesting into whichever graph happened to be open is the most common way to end up with two half-graphs about one subject." ;
    kpred:page          "/ingest" ;
    kpred:part-of       path:AddToExisting ;
    kpred:step-order    "1"^^xsd:integer .

path:Add-2
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    rdfs:label          "Watch the diff, not the fact list" ;
    skos:definition     "After extraction you are shown what CHANGES, not everything found: new facts, facts that contradict something you already confirmed, and near-duplicates. The contradictions are the valuable part — a second source disagreeing with the first is the exact signal the review queue exists for, and it should be settled rather than silently overwritten." ;
    kpred:page          "/compare" ;
    kpred:uses          feat:Compare ;
    kpred:part-of       path:AddToExisting ;
    kpred:step-order    "2"^^xsd:integer .

path:Add-3
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    rdfs:label          "Decide what is the same thing" ;
    skos:definition     "Two names that mean one thing get merged; a shared name-head with different tails (node attribute name / value / type) means they are SIBLINGS and must not be merged. Merging keeps both names — the alternate is preserved as an alias rather than discarded." ;
    kpred:uses          feat:Disambiguation ;
    kpred:part-of       path:AddToExisting ;
    kpred:step-order    "3"^^xsd:integer .

## ===========================================================================
## PATH 3 — Comparing your graph to someone else's
## ===========================================================================

path:CompareGraphs
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    rdfs:label          "Comparing your graph to another's" ;
    skos:definition     "Two people investigated the same subject and reached different graphs. The interesting output is not the overlap — it is what one holds that the other does not, and the places where both hold a claim and the claims conflict." ;
    kpred:audience      "Collaborators, reviewers, a team checking its own consensus, or a learner checking their understanding against an expert's graph." ;
    kpred:starts-with   "Your graph, and someone else's exported graph file." ;
    kpred:ends-with     "A list of genuine disagreements, separated from mere differences of wording." ;
    kpred:uses          feat:Compare ;
    kpred:uses          feat:DiffSummary ;
    kpred:uses          feat:MultiKB ;
    kpred:has-status    "functional" ;
    kpred:honest-note   "THE HARD PART IS TELLING A REAL DISAGREEMENT FROM A NAMING ONE, and that is not fully solved. Two graphs can say the same thing in different words and look like a conflict, or use one word for two things and look like agreement. Predicate matching by embedding was benchmarked across five models and does not separate synonyms reliably (16.7% caught, negative separation), so wording differences are surfaced for a human rather than resolved automatically." ;
    kpred:diagram       """flowchart LR
  A[("Your graph")] --> C["Compare"]
  B[("Theirs, imported<br/>as its own graph")] --> C
  C --> S["Summary first"]
  S --> D["Agreements &middot; conflicts<br/>&middot; only-in-one"]
  D --> T["Take what you agree with"]
  D --> K["Keep the rest visible"]""" ;
    kpred:diagram-caption "Their graph is never merged into yours. Disagreement stays legible instead of being averaged away." ;
    skos:broader        path:UserPaths ;
    hnav:layer          "1"^^xsd:integer ;
    hnav:order          "5"^^xsd:integer .

path:Compare-1
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    rdfs:label          "Bring the other graph in as its own graph" ;
    skos:definition     "Import their export as a SEPARATE graph rather than merging it into yours. Merging first destroys the very thing you wanted to see: whose graph said what." ;
    kpred:page          "/kb" ;
    kpred:uses          feat:MultiKB ;
    kpred:part-of       path:CompareGraphs ;
    kpred:step-order    "1"^^xsd:integer .

path:Compare-2
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    rdfs:label          "Read the summary before the rows" ;
    skos:definition     "The diff summary says what KIND of difference you are looking at — mostly additions, mostly conflicts, mostly rewording — so you know whether you are catching up on their research or arguing with it." ;
    kpred:page          "/compare" ;
    kpred:uses          feat:DiffSummary ;
    kpred:part-of       path:CompareGraphs ;
    kpred:step-order    "2"^^xsd:integer .

path:Compare-3
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    rdfs:label          "Take what you agree with, keep the rest visible" ;
    skos:definition     "Accepting their fact does not erase yours. A conflict that stays recorded as a conflict is more useful than a resolution nobody can audit later — especially when the two of you turn out to have been reading different sources." ;
    kpred:uses          feat:Compare ;
    kpred:part-of       path:CompareGraphs ;
    kpred:step-order    "3"^^xsd:integer .

## ===========================================================================
## PATH 4 — Viewing a story built on a graph
## ===========================================================================

path:ViewStory
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    rdfs:label          "Viewing a story built on a graph" ;
    skos:definition     "The learner's path, and the only one that does not require building anything. Someone who knows a subject has arranged a graph into an ordered walk; you follow it, and the layout changes underneath you as the kind of question changes." ;
    kpred:audience      "Learners, students, anyone handed a graph by an expert. The receiving half of the sharing story." ;
    kpred:starts-with   "A graph someone else built, and no obligation to edit it." ;
    kpred:ends-with     "Having understood a subject through its structure rather than through prose about it." ;
    kpred:uses          feat:StorySystem ;
    kpred:uses          feat:Graph3D ;
    kpred:uses          feat:Shelly ;
    kpred:has-status    "functional" ;
    kpred:example       "static/starter-turtles.ttl — kb:turtle-story, seven steps across six layouts. Each step asks a question the current view cannot answer, then switches to the layout that can, so layouts arrive as answers rather than as features." ;
    kpred:honest-note   "STORIES ARE AUTHORED BY HAND TODAY. There is no story editor: the steps are written as triples in the graph file. That is fine for a starter graph shipped with the app and a real barrier for a teacher who wants to build one for their class." ;
    kpred:diagram       """flowchart LR
  T["Start in the tangle<br/>on purpose"] --> L["Each question picks<br/>its own layout"]
  L --> A["Ask Shelly about<br/>any node you land on"]
  A --> E["End on what the<br/>graph does not know"]""" ;
    kpred:diagram-caption "A story is a route through a graph, not a slideshow. It ends on an absence deliberately." ;
    skos:broader        path:UserPaths ;
    hnav:layer          "1"^^xsd:integer ;
    hnav:order          "6"^^xsd:integer .

path:Story-1
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    rdfs:label          "Start in the tangle on purpose" ;
    skos:definition     "A good story opens on the unarranged graph. Seeing the mess first is what makes the second step land — the tree was always in there, it just could not be seen." ;
    kpred:part-of       path:ViewStory ;
    kpred:step-order    "1"^^xsd:integer .

path:Story-2
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    rdfs:label          "Let each question pick its own layout" ;
    skos:definition     "'How are these related' is a hierarchy question. 'Where does this happen' is a map question. 'In what order' is a timeline question. A story that changes layout to show off layouts teaches nothing; one that changes because the question changed teaches what layouts are for." ;
    kpred:uses          feat:Graph3D ;
    kpred:part-of       path:ViewStory ;
    kpred:step-order    "2"^^xsd:integer .

path:Story-3
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    rdfs:label          "Ask Shelly about any node you land on" ;
    skos:definition     "A story is a path through a graph, not a slideshow — you can stop and ask about anything you pass, and the answer is grounded in the graph rather than in the model's memory." ;
    kpred:uses          feat:Shelly ;
    kpred:part-of       path:ViewStory ;
    kpred:step-order    "3"^^xsd:integer .

path:Story-4
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    rdfs:label          "End on what the graph does not know" ;
    skos:definition     "The last step of a good story is an open question, not a summary. It is the difference between having been told a subject and being able to work in it." ;
    kpred:part-of       path:ViewStory ;
    kpred:step-order    "4"^^xsd:integer .

## ===========================================================================
## PATH 5 — Using reckoning
## ===========================================================================

path:UseReckoning
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    rdfs:label          "Using reckoning to decide something" ;
    skos:definition     "You have a graph and a decision. Reckoning reads the graph as SITUATION (where things stand), TARGET (where you want to be) and PROPOSAL (what would close the gap) — grounded in the facts you confirmed, not in what a model recalls about the subject." ;
    kpred:audience      "Anyone at the point where research has to become a decision." ;
    kpred:starts-with   "A graph with enough confirmed facts to reason over, and a target you can state." ;
    kpred:ends-with     "A proposal you can trace back to the specific facts it rests on." ;
    kpred:uses          feat:Reckoning ;
    kpred:uses          feat:Graph3D ;
    kpred:has-status    "functional" ;
    kpred:honest-note   "IT IS ONLY AS GOOD AS WHAT YOU CONFIRMED. Reckoning reasons over the graph, so a graph full of unreviewed pending facts produces a confident proposal resting on claims nobody checked. Run it after a review pass, not instead of one. It states which facts it used, so a proposal you distrust can be traced rather than argued with." ;
    kpred:diagram       """flowchart LR
  S["Situation<br/>where you actually are"] --> P["Proposal"]
  T["Target<br/>you could tell you reached"] --> P
  G[("Confirmed facts<br/>only")] --> P
  P --> E["Read it against<br/>its evidence"]
  E -.->|"leans on something<br/>you never confirmed"| G""" ;
    kpred:diagram-caption "A reckoning cites the facts it used, so any part of it can be followed back and disagreed with." ;
    skos:broader        path:UserPaths ;
    hnav:layer          "1"^^xsd:integer ;
    hnav:order          "7"^^xsd:integer .

path:Reckon-1
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    rdfs:label          "Say where you actually are" ;
    skos:definition     "The situation is drawn from the graph, but you frame which part of it matters. A reckoning over an entire graph answers a question nobody asked." ;
    kpred:page          "/reckoning" ;
    kpred:uses          feat:Reckoning ;
    kpred:part-of       path:UseReckoning ;
    kpred:step-order    "1"^^xsd:integer .

path:Reckon-2
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    rdfs:label          "State a target you could tell you had reached" ;
    skos:definition     "A target that cannot be checked produces a proposal that cannot be evaluated. 'Choose a platform by Friday' works; 'improve our tooling' does not." ;
    kpred:page          "/reckoning" ;
    kpred:part-of       path:UseReckoning ;
    kpred:step-order    "2"^^xsd:integer .

path:Reckon-3
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    rdfs:label          "Read the proposal against its evidence" ;
    skos:definition     "Follow the facts the proposal cites back into the graph. If it leans on something you never confirmed, that is not a bug in the reckoning — it is the graph telling you what to go and check." ;
    kpred:uses          feat:Reckoning ;
    kpred:part-of       path:UseReckoning ;
    kpred:step-order    "3"^^xsd:integer .

## ===========================================================================
## WHERE THE PATHS MEET
## ===========================================================================

path:PathsOverlap
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    rdfs:label          "The paths are not separate products" ;
    skos:definition     "One person moves between all five as their work changes: research until there is enough to decide, reckon to decide it, compare against a colleague who disagreed, then turn the settled result into a story for whoever comes next. The graph is the same object throughout — that continuity is the point of the whole app." ;
    kpred:principle     "THE SHARING DIRECTION IS RESEARCHER -> LEARNER, AND IT ONLY WORKS IF REVIEW HAPPENED. A story built on unreviewed facts teaches someone a graph nobody checked, with all the authority of a finished artifact and none of the verification. Review is not admin overhead in front of the fun part; it is what makes the sharing honest." ;
    skos:broader        path:UserPaths ;
    hnav:layer          "1"^^xsd:integer ;
    hnav:order          "8"^^xsd:integer .

## ===========================================================================
## PATH 0 — The core loop.  THIS IS THE ONE TO READ FIRST.
## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## Grounded in tests/e2e/workflow-add-review-reckon.test.ts, which drives exactly
## these five stages against the running app on every CI run. If this page and
## that test ever disagree, the test is right and this page is stale.
## ===========================================================================

path:CoreLoop
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    rdfs:label          "The core loop — add, review, add, review, decide" ;
    skos:definition     "The whole product in one path: facts go in, a person settles what is true, and the settled graph is then asked to help decide something. Every other path on this page is this loop with the weight moved to a different stage." ;
    kpred:audience      "Everyone. Read this before any of the specific paths — they will not make sense without it." ;
    kpred:starts-with   "Something you read or wrote, and no graph yet." ;
    kpred:ends-with     "A decision you can trace back to the individual claims it rests on." ;
    kpred:diagram       """flowchart LR
  A1["Add<br/>first note"] --> R1{"Review"}
  R1 --> A2["Add<br/>second note"]
  A2 --> R2{"Review"}
  R2 --> K["Reckon"]
  K --> O["Situation<br/>Target<br/>Proposal"]""" ;
    kpred:diagram-caption "The five stages of tests/e2e/workflow-add-review-reckon.test.ts. The loop repeats: it is add-review-add-review, not add-add-review." ;
    kpred:principle     "REVIEW HAPPENS TWICE, AND THAT IS NOT AN ACCIDENT. Settling the first batch before adding the second is what lets the second batch be matched against something. A graph reviewed only at the end is a pile of claims that never got the chance to connect." ;
    kpred:principle     "NOTHING IS AUTO-ACCEPTED. Every extracted fact arrives PENDING and stays there until a person confirms it. That single rule is the difference between a graph you can rely on and a transcript of what a language model guessed." ;
    kpred:uses          feat:Ingest ;
    kpred:uses          feat:ReviewSystem ;
    kpred:uses          feat:Reckoning ;
    kpred:has-status    "functional" ;
    kpred:tested-by     "tests/e2e/workflow-add-review-reckon.test.ts" ;
    kpred:honest-note   "THIS PATH IS TESTED END TO END ON EVERY CI RUN, against the real app with a mock extraction backend. What the test does NOT cover is extraction QUALITY — it asserts that facts arrive, are settled by a person, and reach the reckoning, not that the facts were the right ones. Quality is measured separately and honestly: see the extraction score, which currently tops out at 74% recall on a hand-checked corpus." ;
    skos:broader        path:UserPaths ;
    hnav:layer          "1"^^xsd:integer ;
    hnav:order          "0"^^xsd:integer .

path:Core-1
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    rdfs:label          "Add — put something in" ;
    skos:definition     "Paste a note, drop a file, give it a URL, or say it out loud. The app reads it and proposes triples: separate claims, each small enough to judge on its own. You are not writing the graph by hand — you are giving it something to read." ;
    kpred:page          "/ingest" ;
    kpred:uses          feat:Ingest ;
    kpred:part-of       path:CoreLoop ;
    kpred:step-order    "1"^^xsd:integer .

path:Core-2
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    rdfs:label          "Review — settle what is actually true" ;
    skos:definition     "The proposed facts are waiting, not accepted. Confirm the ones that are right, reject the ones that are not, and leave a question where the source did not actually say. This is the step people want to skip and the step that makes everything after it worth having." ;
    kpred:page          "/review" ;
    kpred:uses          feat:ReviewSystem ;
    kpred:part-of       path:CoreLoop ;
    kpred:step-order    "2"^^xsd:integer .

path:Core-3
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    rdfs:label          "Add again — and watch it connect" ;
    skos:definition     "The second source is where a graph starts being more than a list. Names that match something already settled attach to it; names that do not stand alone as new. That matching only works because you settled the first batch first." ;
    kpred:page          "/ingest" ;
    kpred:uses          feat:EntityNormalization ;
    kpred:part-of       path:CoreLoop ;
    kpred:step-order    "3"^^xsd:integer .

path:Core-4
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    rdfs:label          "Review again — now with something to compare against" ;
    skos:definition     "The second review is different from the first: the app can now tell you this claim agrees with one you already accepted, contradicts it, or is about something new. A disagreement surfaced here is the most valuable thing the graph produces." ;
    kpred:page          "/review" ;
    kpred:uses          feat:ReviewSystem ;
    kpred:uses          feat:DiffSummary ;
    kpred:part-of       path:CoreLoop ;
    kpred:step-order    "4"^^xsd:integer .

path:Core-5
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    rdfs:label          "Reckon — ask the graph to help you decide" ;
    skos:definition     "Say where you are, say what you are trying to reach, and the graph proposes a route using only facts you confirmed. It cites them, so you can follow any part of the proposal back to the claim underneath it and disagree with that claim specifically." ;
    kpred:page          "/reckoning" ;
    kpred:uses          feat:Reckoning ;
    kpred:part-of       path:CoreLoop ;
    kpred:step-order    "5"^^xsd:integer .

## ===========================================================================
## PATH 6 — Keeping everyday notes
## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## Added 2026-09-06 after feedback from a first-time reader who arrived expecting
## a note-taking and storyboarding app and could not find the thing that makes
## this different. That reaction is USEFUL DATA, not a misunderstanding to correct
## away: if the condensation step is not visible on the way in, what remains looks
## like a notes app with extra work in the middle.
## ===========================================================================

path:EverydayNotes
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    rdfs:label          "Keeping everyday notes" ;
    skos:definition     "You want to write things down the way you always have — loosely, in your own words, without deciding up front what any of it means. The difference here is what happens next: the note is kept as you wrote it AND read for the separate claims inside it, so later you can ask what you actually know rather than search for a paragraph you half remember." ;
    kpred:audience      "Anyone who takes notes. The most common way in, and the least well served today." ;
    kpred:starts-with   "A thought, in your own words, with no structure imposed on it." ;
    kpred:ends-with     "The note kept verbatim, plus the claims inside it settled as facts you can query and connect." ;
    kpred:diagram       """flowchart LR
  W["You write<br/>freely"] --> K["Note kept<br/>verbatim"]
  W --> E["Read for<br/>claims"]
  E --> P["Pending facts"]
  P --> R{"You settle<br/>them"}
  R --> G[("Graph")]
  K -.->|"always recoverable"| G""" ;
    kpred:diagram-caption "The note survives as written; the claims inside it become separately reviewable. The dotted line is the promise that structuring never costs you the original." ;
    kpred:principle     "THE PROSE IS NEVER THE LOSER. Whatever the extractor makes of a note, the note itself is kept verbatim — fillers, comma splices and all. A system that replaced your sentence with its own reading of it would be asking you to trust a summary of your own thought." ;
    kpred:principle     "TRIPLES STAY THE CORE, AND FREEFORM IS THE WAY IN, NOT AN ALTERNATIVE TO THEM. Storing prose and calling it done is the easy version and it forfeits everything downstream: you cannot diff a paragraph, cite half of it, notice it contradicts another one, or record that it left a question open. Freeform capture is worth supporting because typing triples by hand is a terrible way to think — not because the triple was the problem." ;
    kpred:uses          feat:Ingest ;
    kpred:uses          feat:ReviewSystem ;
    kpred:has-status    "scaffolded" ;
    kpred:honest-note   "READ THE STATUS. You can write a freeform note today at /ingest and it will be extracted into reviewable facts — that part works. What does NOT exist is a note-taking SURFACE: no notebook, no daily page, no editing a note after you wrote it, no browsing your notes as notes rather than as graph nodes. Today a note is an input to extraction and then it is a source record. If you arrived wanting somewhere to keep your thinking, that place is not built yet." ;
    kpred:open-question "What is the unit a person sees — the note, or the claims taken out of it? The review queue chose the claim and became unusable at scale; the answer for everyday notes may be the opposite, with claims folded underneath the sentence that produced them." ;
    skos:broader        path:UserPaths ;
    hnav:layer          "1"^^xsd:integer ;
    hnav:order          "1"^^xsd:integer .

path:Everyday-1
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    rdfs:label          "Write it the way you would have anyway" ;
    skos:definition     "No template, no fields, no deciding what kind of note this is. Structure is something the app proposes afterwards, and something you approve — never a form you fill in before you are allowed to write." ;
    kpred:page          "/ingest" ;
    kpred:part-of       path:EverydayNotes ;
    kpred:step-order    "1"^^xsd:integer .

path:Everyday-2
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    rdfs:label          "Let it find the claims you made without noticing" ;
    skos:definition     "A sentence usually asserts more than one thing. Splitting them apart is the step that turns a note into something answerable later — and it is the step you would never do by hand, which is the entire reason a model is involved at all." ;
    kpred:uses          feat:Ingest ;
    kpred:part-of       path:EverydayNotes ;
    kpred:step-order    "2"^^xsd:integer .

path:Everyday-3
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    rdfs:label          "Correct it, because it will be wrong sometimes" ;
    skos:definition     "Extraction is measured, not assumed: the best model tested recovers about three quarters of what a careful reader would. Review is where that gap gets closed, and it is why the facts wait for you instead of being written straight into the graph." ;
    kpred:page          "/review" ;
    kpred:uses          feat:ReviewSystem ;
    kpred:part-of       path:EverydayNotes ;
    kpred:step-order    "3"^^xsd:integer ;
    kpred:honest-note   "The measured number is real and is published rather than rounded up: 74% strict recall on a hand-checked corpus, with the previously configured model scoring 22%. Assume you will be correcting things." .

## ===========================================================================
## PATH 7 — Capturing a thought you did not sit down to write
## ===========================================================================

path:CaptureSpoken
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    rdfs:label          "Capturing something you said out loud" ;
    skos:definition     "The thought arrives while you are walking, driving, or away from the machine. You say it; it reaches the graph as pending facts you settle later, at a desk, when you have the attention to judge it." ;
    kpred:audience      "Anyone whose ideas do not wait for a keyboard." ;
    kpred:starts-with   "A sentence said into a phone or a wearable, nowhere near the app." ;
    kpred:ends-with     "Pending facts waiting in review the next time you open the graph." ;
    kpred:diagram       """flowchart LR
  V["You speak"] --> S["Phone shortcut<br/>or wearable"]
  S --> H["Your own n8n<br/>(holds it)"]
  H --> F["Shared folder"]
  F --> A["App picks it up<br/>on a 10s poll"]
  A --> R{"Review<br/>when you are back"}""" ;
    kpred:diagram-caption "n8n is the buffer that makes this work: a note spoken while the laptop is shut has to live somewhere until the app next runs." ;
    kpred:principle     "A DICTATED INSTRUCTION IS NOT A DICTATED FACT. Saying 'find me three suppliers' is a REQUEST, and an extractor with no notion of that will happily record it as a claim that three suppliers exist. Requests are routed into tasks before the model sees them, and a dictated task lands with no permissions and no acceptance test — so speech is never a route to unsupervised action." ;
    kpred:uses          feat:Ingest ;
    kpred:uses          feat:ReviewSystem ;
    kpred:uses          feat:WhisperSTT ;
    kpred:has-status    "scaffolded" ;
    kpred:honest-note   "THE HONEST LIMIT: A FOLDER CANNOT WAKE A CLOSED TAB. The app is a static site in a browser, so the poll runs only while it is open. Notes accumulate in the shared folder and arrive when you next open the app — they are not lost, but they are not live either. True background capture needs a process rather than a page, and that is not built. The n8n workflows that make the phone half work are also not merged yet." ;
    skos:broader        path:UserPaths ;
    hnav:layer          "1"^^xsd:integer ;
    hnav:order          "2"^^xsd:integer .
